 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+- **Change delivery over self-hosted WebSockets.** Committed revisions reach browsers over persistent WebSocket sessions, with the change store retaining replayable revisions and an outbox for reliable publication and browsers running reconnect and catch-up. Five-second HTTPS polling and a hosted realtime service were rejected: polling adds repeated reads and a few seconds of lag, and a hosted service adds recurring fees and provider coupling. Self-hosted sockets give fast updates with delivery operated in-house, at the cost of owning connection capacity and reconnect handling. See `docs/decisions/change-delivery-approach.md` for the options, criteria, and rationale.
